check_coins() never returns anything - it only print()s inside itself and implicitly returns None. Your last line, print(check_coins()), first runs the function (which prints the amount due) and then prints its return value, which is None. That extra None is what you're seeing.

Fix: either call the function without wrapping it in print(), since it already prints internally:

check_coins()

or, better, make the function return a value and print once at the call site:

def check_coins():
    if 0 <= insert < 50:
        return f"Amount Due: {amount_due}"
    elif insert > 50:
        return insert - 50
    else:
        return f"Amount Due: {amount_due}"

print(check_coins())

Either approach removes the stray None line.

Panel's notifications are rendered by the Notyf JS library outside your normal component tree, so pn.Column(width=...) can't reach them - but .bk-notification isn't a real Notyf class name, which is why your CSS never applied. Target Notyf's actual classes instead:

pn.extension(notifications=True, raw_css=[
.notyf__toast {
    max-width: 600px !important;
}
.notyf__message {
    white-space: normal !important;
    word-break: break-word !important;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere !important;
}
])

Also check the pn.extension(...) call itself: the raw_css list isn't closed with a matching ) before the next statement in the snippet you posted, which would raise a SyntaxError on its own and could explain earlier failures. Reload the page after adding CSS, since Panel injects extension-level styles at load time, not per notification call.

A generator raises StopIteration once its underlying code finishes - the for loop in sensor_data_generator ends after n=5 iterations, so next() has nothing left to produce. A for loop catches StopIteration for you automatically, which is why your second version just stops cleanly; calling next() manually means you must handle it yourself.

Two fixes:

1) Catch the exception:
for _ in range(6):
    try:
        print(next(sensor_stream))
    except StopIteration:
        print("generator exhausted")
        break

2) Give next() a default sentinel so it returns that instead of raising:
for _ in range(6):
    val = next(sensor_stream, None)
    if val is None:
        break
    print(val)

Prefer option 2 when you just want to stop gracefully, and option 1 when you need to distinguish "exhausted" from a legitimate falsy reading.

header("Location:index.html") uses a relative path, and PHP resolves it relative to the current script's URL directory. Since the script lives at /scripts/wol.php, the browser gets redirected to /scripts/index.html, which doesn't exist - hence the 404. Use a site-root-absolute path instead:

header("Location: /index.html");
exit;

Also, your Python script's stdout is captured by system() and echoed directly into the page before header() runs; if any bytes get flushed early, PHP will emit "headers already sent" and the redirect silently fails. Buffer or discard that output:

ob_start();
system("python /var/www/html/scripts/python/wake.py ...");
ob_end_clean();
header("Location: /index.html");
exit;

Separately, <button><a href=...> is invalid HTML - interactive elements can't be nested. Use a plain <a> styled as a button, or a <form> submitting to the PHP script.

There's no safe way to forcibly pause or kill an arbitrary Python thread from the outside - the interpreter doesn't expose that, which is why acquiring a lock in stop() does nothing to a thread stuck inside someone else's callback that never checks that lock. Cooperative signaling is the only reliable mechanism: the callback itself must periodically check a shared flag/Event, so this only works if you control (or can wrap) the loop inside the callback.

import threading

class Thread:
    def __init__(self, callback, *args):
        self._pause = threading.Event()
        self._pause.set()
        self._stop = threading.Event()
        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._run, args=(callback, args))
        self._thread.start()

    def _run(self, callback, args):
        while not self._stop.is_set():
            self._pause.wait()
            callback(*args)

    def stop(self):
        self._stop.set()
        self._pause.set()

    def pause(self):
        self._pause.clear()

If the user's function genuinely can't check flags, threads can't stop it externally - only multiprocessing.Process.terminate() can kill it, at the cost of losing shared state.

The recursive calls never actually change x or y - and worse, x and y get overwritten to given_map[0][0] on every single call, discarding whatever was passed in, so you truly loop forever on the same arguments. You need to treat each cell's value as the fixed number of steps to move right or down from there, and recurse into the new position:

def path(given_map, x, y):
    n = len(given_map)
    if x == n - 1 and y == n - 1:
        return 1
    if x >= n or y >= n:
        return 0
    step = given_map[y][x]
    total = 0
    if x + step < n:
        total += path(given_map, x + step, y)
    if y + step < n:
        total += path(given_map, x, y + step)
    return total

print(path(input_map, 0, 0))

Remove the two lines that reassign x = given_map[0][0] and y = given_map[0][0] at the top of the function - that's the line silently breaking every recursive call.
